Pharyngitis
Opthalmology & ENT
Severe sore throat, Pharyngeal erythema/exudate, Tender cervical lymphadenopathy

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OET-035
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe throat pain and high fever resulting in fluid refusal and profound systemic rigors.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported an acute onset of severe odynophagia, high-grade fever, and a toxic clinical appearance in a previously healthy adult. Urgent assessment was required to definitively differentiate group A streptococcal pharyngitis (requiring rapid antibiotic initiation to prevent acute rheumatic fever and peritonsillar abscess) from viral etiologies or life-threatening epiglottitis, managing the immediate risk of severe dehydration prior to routine hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "Swallowing feels like I am eating broken glass, I am freezing cold despite sweating, and my throat is coated in white slime."
History of Present Illness: Acute onset of severe sore throat and pain upon swallowing (odynophagia) commencing 24 hours ago. Rapidly escalated to include high fever (39.0C) accompanied by profound systemic chills and rigors. Patient notes a distinct absence of cough, coryza, and conjunctivitis. Endorses accompanying severe halitosis and a generalized toxic, acutely ill clinical feeling. Unable to tolerate solid foods; fluid intake is minimal due to extreme pain.
Functional Status: Bedbound, acutely fatigued, limiting speech due to pharyngeal pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; high risk of dehydration due to voluntary fluid restriction.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/75 | HR: 112 | Temp: 39.1 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 80 Kg
General: Toxic, acutely ill appearance, diaphoretic, experiencing visible rigors during the exam.
Physical Exam: Oropharyngeal examination reveals a markedly erythematous and visibly edematous posterior pharyngeal mucosal wall. Patchy, discrete, whitish-yellow purulent exudate explicitly covers the tonsils and posterior pharynx. Petechiae are densely distributed across the hard and soft palate (palatal petechiae). Strawberry tongue characterized by prominent, severely edematous red papillae is present. Tender, palpably enlarged anterior cervical lymph nodes are noted bilaterally. Uvula is midline, no severe unilateral soft palate swelling. Airway is patent without stridor.
Environmental Observations: Heavy blankets piled on the bed; heating turned up high despite the fever.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Nil regular medications. No known drug allergies.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Bacterial Pharyngitis (Suspected Group A Streptococcus) - Status: Acute, severe
Differentials:

Viral Pharyngitis/URI - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absolute absence of cough and coryza, combined with high fever, dense purulent exudate, and palatal petechiae (Modified Centor Score of 4, highly predictive of bacterial etiology).

Infectious Mononucleosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The sudden, hyper-acute onset of symptoms (mono is typically insidious), and the predominant finding of anterior rather than posterior cervical lymphadenopathy, lacking hepatosplenomegaly.

Peritonsillar Abscess - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Symmetric tonsillar swelling, midline uvula, and absence of trismus, ruling out a deep space collection.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with a textbook, hyper-acute bacterial pharyngitis, scoring maximum points on the Centor criteria, strongly indicating Group A Streptococcus. Urgent face-to-face assessment was necessary to physically rule out dangerous localized complications (quinsy) or airway emergencies (epiglottitis), which was achieved via direct visualization of a symmetric, patent, but heavily infected oropharynx. Because of the extreme severity of the odynophagia leading to fluid refusal and systemic toxicity, immediate prescription of targeted antibiotics and aggressive, structured antipyretic/analgesic therapy was required to prevent clinical deterioration and emergency department admission for IV rehydration overnight.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed oral Phenoxymethylpenicillin 500mg QID for 10 days to definitively eradicate the strep carriage and prevent rheumatic complications. Advised oral Paracetamol 1g QID and Ibuprofen 400mg TDS for aggressive fever and pain control.
Positive Behavioral Change: To ensure adequate hydration despite the severe pain, the patient must set a 15-minute timer and consume exactly one small ice cube or 30mL of icy water each time it rings. The intense, localized cooling temporarily numbs the inflamed mucosa, providing immediate positive reinforcement for swallowing, breaking the cycle of fluid refusal and systematically rehydrating the patient without requiring large, painful gulps.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 48 hours to ensure response to antibiotics and rule out abscess formation.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the patient develops an absolute inability to swallow saliva (drooling), extreme difficulty opening the mouth, a muffled 'hot potato' voice, or any difficulty breathing.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; swab for culture is recommended but treatment is initiated empirically due to high pre-test probability.
Clinical Course: The high fever and agonizing throat pain will begin to subside within 24-48 hours of starting the penicillin. You remain contagious until you have been on the antibiotics for a full 24 hours.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

If your jaw locks up, your voice changes to sound thick and muffled, or you find you cannot even swallow your own spit, you must proceed to the emergency department immediately.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: You have a severe bacterial infection (Strep throat) causing massive inflammation in your throat. The penicillin is necessary to kill the bacteria and prevent it from damaging your heart or kidneys later. The pain relief will lower your fever and make swallowing possible.
Actionable Steps: Take the full 10-day course of penicillin even when you feel completely better. Remove the heavy blankets to let your fever break. Stick strictly to the 15-minute ice-cube rule to stay hydrated.

